--expect
65530 65529 | 1010 | 0 -1 140737488355327 -140737488355328 | 4660 0 65529 | 4660 65529
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE LAYOUT, not recorded. A value is a 16-bit TAG over a 48-bit
PAYLOAD; `TAG_FIXNUM` is 0xFFFA = 65530 and `TAG_HEAP` is 0xFFF9 = 65529.

 1-2. `65530 65529` -- each constructor's tag survives its own round trip. A
      shift of the wrong width puts the tag somewhere else entirely and both
      of these change together, which is why both are here.
 3-6. `1010` -- the bound at both ends. `FIXNUM_MAX` fits and one past does
      not; `FIXNUM_MIN` fits and one past does not. The bottom is INCLUSIVE
      where the top is exclusive, two's complement having one more negative
      than positive, so a symmetrically written bound answers `1011`.
 7-10. The fixnum round trip. `0` and the two bounds are the easy half; `-1`
      is the case with teeth. A negative is all ones above bit 47, so WITHOUT
      the mask in `make-fixnum` those bits land in the TAG and the value reads
      back as some other kind entirely -- and without the sign extension in
      `fixnum-payload`, `-1` answers 281474976710655 instead.
11-13. `4660 0 65529` -- the heap round trip, then 2^48, which is ONE PAST the
      payload field. This is the exact input that separated the three
      runtimes: both ports masked it to 0, native ORed it into the tag. The
      tag must still read 65529 afterwards.
14-15. `4660 65529` -- an address with both halves set. The low bits must
      survive and the tag must be untouched. Case 12 alone would pass an
      implementation that returned 0 for everything; this one will not.
